===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|Indias}}

# {{senseid|en|Q668}}{{place|en|country|r/South Asia|capital=New Delhi|official=Republic of India}}.
{{syn|en|Hindiyyah|Hindustan|Bharat}}
# {{senseid|en|region}}{{lb|en|chiefly|historical|proscribed|_|in modern use}} {{place|en|region|r/South Asia|traditionally delimited by the [[Himalayas]] and the [[Indus]] river; the [[Indian subcontinent]]}}.
# {{lb|en|historical|often "British India"}} A territory of the British Empire, chiefly comprising the modern day countries of India, [[Pakistan]], [[Bangladesh]], [[Bhutan]], [[Nepal]] and [[Burma]].
# {{given name|en|female}}

====Usage notes====
When discussing the pre-modern history of South Asia, naming the entire subcontinent ''India'' is common. For discussing the current states, which include the Republic of India, this can cause offense and confusion and is usually avoided with the terms ''Indian Subcontinent'' or ''South Asia'' (except in the specific context of discussing {{w|Indian reunification}}).

At times when multiple countries existed in South Asia, these have sometimes been pluralised as ''[[Indias]]'' or ''[[Indies]]''. The word ''Indies'' survives in some place names such as ''[[East Indies]]'' and ''[[West Indies]]'' but is otherwise obsolete.

